A lambda expression is basically a short form of writing an anonymous method (a method without a name).

It’s Java’s way of writing code that behaves like a function and can be passed around as data.

Syntax:
(parameter) -> expression


Main Use / Purpose of Lambda Expressions:

Make code shorter and cleaner
Enable use of functional interfaces (interfaces with one abstract method)
Allow you to pass behavior (functions) as arguments
Support the new Streams API

Core uses of Lambda expressions:

| Purpose                                    | Explanation                                 | Example                                                  |
| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| **1. Implement Functional Interfaces**     | Replace anonymous classes                   | `Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Run");`          |
| **2. Make Stream operations concise**      | Used in `.map()`, `.filter()`, `.forEach()` | `list.stream().filter(s -> s.startsWith("S")).toList();` |
| **3. Pass behavior as parameter**          | Methods can take “functions”                | `doWork(() -> System.out.println("Work done"));`         |
| **4. Enable functional-style programming** | Cleaner and expressive code                 | `names.forEach(n -> System.out.println(n));`             |


Summary:
| Aspect            | Explanation                                                     |
| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Introduced in** | Java 8                                                          |
| **Purpose**       | To simplify code and enable functional programming              |
| **Replaces**      | Anonymous inner classes                                         |
| **Works with**    | Functional interfaces                                           |
| **Common uses**   | Streams (`map`, `filter`, `forEach`), event handling, threading |
| **Syntax**        | `(parameters) -> expression`                                    |
